    "q-36355": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36355",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ If ${b}_{1}=5{c}_{2}-3{c}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2},$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C.$",
      "worked_solution": "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
